    "narrative": "Our offensive outlook for today is headlined by the trio of **Taylor Ward** (67.28), **Luis Arraez** (67.31), and **Matt Chapman** (66.77). **Taylor Ward** earns the nod as our strongest play, buoyed by a favorable matchup against the hittable Griffin Canning; despite a minor platoon disadvantage, the combination of a high-leadoff spot and a 16% park-factor boost creates a high-ceiling environment for counting stats. **Luis Arraez** and **Matt Chapman** benefit from a projected soft landing against Javier Assad, whose 4.63 xERA suggests our core middle-order bats should find success in high-leverage spots. **Gavin Sheets** also makes a compelling case with a score of 64.98, as he significantly capitalizes on the same park factors benefiting **Taylor Ward**, making him a priority start despite the volatility associated with Shane Baz.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Gleyber Torres**, who posted a 49.70 but was ultimately edged out by the optimization of our outfield and utility slots. While **Gleyber Torres** remains a high-floor asset, he fell victim to a negative platoon split against Tanner Bibee and a lack of the explosive speed metrics we covet in our 5x5 Roto framework. Conversely, **Geraldo Perdomo** (64.80) secured a start despite facing a tougher southpaw in Nick Lodolo, as his base efficiency and favorable order position outweighed the potential upside of **Bryson Stott** (48.48) or **Daulton Varsho** (45.65). **Daulton Varsho** was the primary consideration for a utility spot given his speed profile against a slower delivery, but his current platoon struggles against lefties kept him on the bench.\n\nA recurring theme today is the reliance on \"Lineup Pending\" projections; while we have accounted for traditional batting order spots, any shift in the lineup cards could move the needle for players like **Jonathan Aranda** (55.26), whose score is heavily dependent on maintaining his spot in the heart of the order. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (66.52), as facing Jack Flaherty presents the highest difficulty index among our starters. We are prioritizing volume in the outfield, banking on the collective output of **Taylor Ward**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, **Lawrence Butler**, and **Gavin Sheets** to keep us competitive in the stolen base and run-production categories while keeping **Drew Gilbert** in the final spot for his platoon-adjusted ceiling against Javier Assad.",
